### Jet Reconstruction

The proton beams delivered to STAR are at sufficiently high energies that partons are elastically scattered from each other and ejected from the parent protons. Because of confinement, these ejected partons quickly fragment and hadronize into jets of colorless particles. Studying properties of the jets reveals information about the primordial partons involved in the hard scattering.

To study the properties of jets, they must be reconstructed from the raw tracks in the TPC and energy deposits in the calorimeters. There are multiple jet algorithms that can be applied as a part of the FastJet package in C++ [60]. From these, the anti-\(k_{T}\) jet reconstruction algorithm [61] is used for this analysis.

Figure 3.2: **Average BEMC Tower \(E_{T}\) vs. Run Index - The average BEMC \(E_{T}\) after removing problematic runs.**

